WEBVTT

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.360
Operations for the last servicing mission to the Hubble Space Telescope are well underway

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.960
at several NASA facilities.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.040
Almost all 22,000 pounds of Service Mission 4 hardware is at some stage of integration

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.720
and test at the Goddard Space Flight Center in Maryland.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.540
This includes new gyros.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.300
batteries, fine guidance sensor, astronaut tools, support hardware, a new docking ring,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.830
the carrier systems, and two new Hubble science instruments.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:47.930
The Wide Field Camera 3 begins its third and final thermal vacuum test shortly, where

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:53.590
it will experience the harshness of space inside a large simulator for several weeks.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.620
The service mission for astronauts come to Goddard to practice installing the camera

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.000
into the Hubble Space Telescope high fidelity.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.350
mechanical simulator, as well as the carrier systems that will transport the

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.310
camera to orbit on board the space shuttle. The Wide Field Camera 3 will be

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:13.300
Hubble's most advanced camera, increasing its discovery efficiency by a

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.190
factor of five. The Cosmic Origin spectrograph, a point source ultraviolet

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:24.460
spectrograph, is ready for flight. Astronauts practice installing costs into

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.070
the high fidelity mechanical simulator and the carrier systems.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.260
At Goddard, engineers and astronauts worked together developing tools and techniques to

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.030
resurrect two malfunctioning science instruments currently on orbit inside Hubble.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.630
Both the Space Telescope Imaging Spectrograph and the Advanced Camera for Surveys suffered

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.740
power supply failures.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:55.530
This is the first time a repair like this has ever been done on orbit.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.000
One of NASA's premier training facilities at the Johnson Space Center in Houston is the

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.040
In this 6.2-million-gallon swimming pool, Goddard engineers work alongside the astronauts

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.860
as they train for the mission.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.140
Scientists and engineers are excited about flying this last servicing mission to the

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.150
Hubble Space Telescope, and we'll update you periodically as preparations continue.
